g every task to specific SRD requirements, and a pure contract task doesn't implement anything. For a main package with no exported types, the contract feels hollow. I could merge the contract with the first batch of requirements, but then it stops being a contract. Maybe the better move is to just fold the contract into the first implementation task and skip the separate structural step. I'm realizing the distinction matters here—a contract task should define only the signatures as empty stubs, while implementation tasks fill in the logic. For a cmd/ package, that means the contract would have a bare main() function and stub signatures for helper functions like soak() and writeFile(), leaving the actual logic for subsequent implementation tasks.\n\nNow I'm wrestling with how to anchor the contract task to SRD requirements when it's purely structural. I could tie it to all requirements since it sets up the foundation, or just the initial ones that define the basic contract structure. The constraint requires anchoring to specific requirement IDs, so I need to pick which ones make sense for a pure structural task.\n\nLet me just commit to a plan and move forward—I'll anchor the contract to the foundational requirements that cover argument parsing and basic I/O flow, then map out the remaining implementation tasks with their specific requirement coverage across the releases.\n\nNow I'm verifying the weight constraints for each task to ensure they stay within the four-point limit, and everything checks out. With that validated, I'm ready to write out the YAML task descriptions, starting with what I know about how sponge operates—it buffers stdin into memory before writing to the target file.\n\nLooking at the requirement groups for sponge, I'm mapping out what each category likely covers: the R1 group handles core read-and-write functionality, R2 covers command-line options like append mode, R3 addresses error handling, R4 tackles edge cases, and R5 deals with output behavior or special scenarios.\n\nNow I need to write the YAML carefully, and I've just realized I should verify the file naming convention. The SRD files follow the pattern `docs/specs/software-requirements/srdNNN-name.yaml`, so srd007-sponge would be at that path. The related use cases and test suites would live in their respective directories under `docs/specs/`, organized by release version.\n\nFor the required_reading section, I'll need to reference the SRD file itself, any relevant package contracts like the testutils for differential testing, and the existing code files that are being modified. I'm assuming the weights file mentioned in the commit history is at the repo root, but the stitch agent should be able to locate it—the key is pointing to the SRD for the actual implementation details. I'm wrestling with the P2 constraint about splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks. The existing pipeline combined multiple requirements with their tests together, but P2 is explicit that a single task can't both implement and test the same feature. For a single remaining requirement like cat R5.4, splitting into separate impl and test tasks feels wasteful, but the constraint seems to demand it. I'm realizing the test file serves as the actual verification mechanism here—it runs the Go binary against GNU reference output, so implementation has to come first. Looking back at the actual pipeline pattern in recent commits, it seems like implementation and tests are being combined for simpler tasks. But P2 is explicit: \"Always split implementation and tests into separate tasks\" with no exceptions. So even though cat R5.4 is straightforward, I need to follow that constraint strictly and split it into separate implementation and test tasks. I'm settling on a four-task structure: implementing R5.4 for cat, adding its test, creating the sponge contract, then implementing R1.1-R1.4 for sponge. Task 1 depends on task 0 completing the implementation, and task 3 depends on task 2 setting up the contract file.\n\nActually, I could consolidate the cat work into a single task covering both implementation and testing, which would free up a slot to tackle more of the sponge requirements instead of splitting them across separate tasks. I'm laying out the full task breakdown: the cat command gets a single small task for requirement R5.4, then sponge gets three tasks—first setting up the structural contract, then implementing the first batch of four requirements, and finally the remaining four requirements with a dependency chain to ensure proper ordering.\n\nNow I need to format this as YAML following the existing pipeline convention where task titles match the pattern \"[stitch] cmd/<name> (srdNNN-name R#.#, ...)\". Let me write out the detailed task descriptions so the stitch agent has everything it needs: understanding the requirements from the SRD, reviewing the current implementation in main.go, and checking the test patterns to match the expected behavior.\n\nFor the sponge contract task, I'm realizing it's structural setup rather than requirement implementation—it establishes the file structure and function stubs that subsequent tasks will build on. The contract itself doesn't anchor to specific SRD requirements since it's just scaffolding, but the implementation tasks that follow will each target their own requirement IDs. I'm checking the size constraints—the sponge contract will be small stubs, and the core functionality across R1.1-R1.4 should land around 100-200 lines, which is acceptable even though it's below the 300-500 target. Let me also verify the canonical ordering: documentation before code, types before implementations, libraries before consumers, and implementation before tests—all checks out. Now I'm ready to write the output. Writing the task contract... Looking at the sponge task requirements, I need to set up the main package with SIGPIPE handling, configure all the command-line flags from the spec, and stub out the internal functions for each operation described in the SRD. Now I'm looking at the next task which involves implementing the core sponge operations across multiple requirements from the specification, building on the function stubs I just created in main.go. per the specification. Now I'm moving on to the next phase, which involves implementing additional requirements R1.5 through R2.3 for the sponge command, building on the foundation from the previous task. I'm noticing the task descriptions are too vague and need more context about what sponge actually does and how it should work. Let me revise them to be more self-contained so the stitch agent understands the requirements without needing to read the SRD separately.\n\nI'm also reconsidering whether P2 really applies here—the policy says to split implementation and tests, but also to combine trivial changes. A single requirement addition might qualify as trivial, and the golden example actually shows a combined task with both implementation and test files, so maybe that approach is fine.\n\nI should also think through whether the contract task is actually necessary. here. The sponge utility is simple:\n1. Read all stdin\n2. Write to file (or append with -a)\n3. Handle errors\n\nA contract for this would be ~20 lines of stubs. It might be better to just have the first implementation task create the file.\n\nBut the constraint says \"Do not propose implementation tasks for a package that has no contract task unless the package already exists in source_code.\" I'll keep the contract.\n\nLet me revise the descriptions with more context. I should also make sure the file paths for SRDs are correct. Looking at the SRD naming convention, the architecture specifies `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd[NNN]-[feature-name].yaml`. Checking the project context, I see identifiers like `srd086-hashutil`, `srd087-sizeparse`, `srd088-encutil`, `srd109-dircolors` — all using 3 digits with zero padding. So my file paths sho